\item We characterize the failure of the previous analysis of contrastive learning, and develop a new understanding through the augmentation overlap effect. Compared to existing theories on contrastive learning, ours can provide guidance to the practical designing of contrastive methods and evaluation metrics.
\item We establish general guarantees (both upper and lower bounds) for the downstream performance without assumptions on conditional independence. And we further show how the two bounds could asymptotically converge under our less restrictive assumptions.
\item We provide a quantitative discussion on the effect of augmentation strength, which verifies our theory from both theoretical and empirical aspects. Motivated by our theory, we further propose a new unsupervised evaluation metric for contrastive learning named ARC and show that it aligns well with downstream performance on real-world datasets.
